From 258b02dc5706431d3d2acd5fa7397d46554e372d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Z User <z@container>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 08:50:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Drop /p:TargetFramework global from the msbuild publish
 command
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

MSBuild global properties flow into every project reference during
NuGet restore, so forcing TargetFramework=net8.0-windows10.0.17763.0
made the modules (Localization, UpdateProcessor, Extensions.NET,
HtmlRenderer) restore with the wrong TFM target in their
project.assets.json, while the build phase lets each reference use its
own net6.0-windows TFM — NETSDK1005, no output.

BedrockLauncher.csproj is single-target, so no override is needed.
---
 .github/workflows/linux-appimage.yml | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/.github/workflows/linux-appimage.yml b/.github/workflows/linux-appimage.yml
index 65b99e8..5d71648 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/linux-appimage.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/linux-appimage.yml
@@ -79,11 +79,16 @@ jobs:
       - name: Publish BedrockLauncher
         shell: pwsh
         run: |
+          # NOTE: do NOT pass /p:TargetFramework here. MSBuild global
+          # properties flow into every project reference during restore,
+          # which makes NuGet write the wrong TFM target into the modules'
+          # project.assets.json (their own TFM is net6.0-windows) and the
+          # build then fails with NETSDK1005. BedrockLauncher.csproj is
+          # single-target, so no TFM override is needed.
           msbuild "${env:GITHUB_WORKSPACE}/BedrockLauncher/BedrockLauncher.csproj" `
             /restore `
             /t:Publish `
             /p:Configuration=Release `
-            /p:TargetFramework=net8.0-windows10.0.17763.0 `
             /p:RuntimeIdentifier=win-x64 `
             /p:SelfContained=false `
             /p:PublishDir="${env:GITHUB_WORKSPACE}/publish/win-x64/" `
